{"name":"Selective Yellow","hex":"#ffba00","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"selective yellow","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A standardized color for automotive lamps, intended to improve vision by removing blue wavelengths from the projected light."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/selective_yellow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Selective yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Selective yellow is a colour for automotive lamps, particularly headlamps and other road-illumination lamps, such as fog lamps. Under ECE regulations, headlamps were formerly permitted to be either white or selective yellow—in France, selective yellow was mandatory for all vehicles' road-illumination lamps until 1993."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Corning_Conaphore_y.jpg/330px-Corning_Conaphore_y.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selective_yellow"},{"title":"Yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Yellow is a color between green and orange on the spectrum of light. It is evoked by light with a dominant wavelength of roughly 575–585 nm. It is a primary color in subtractive color systems, used in painting or color printing. In the RGB color model, used to create colors on television and computer screens, yellow is a secondary color made by combining red and green at equal intensity. Carotenoids give the characteristic yellow color to autumn leaves, corn, canaries, daffodils, and lemons, as well as egg yolks, buttercups, and bananas. They absorb light energy and protect plants from photo damage in some cases. Sunlight has a slight yellowish hue when the Sun is near the horizon, due to atmospheric scattering of shorter wavelengths."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg/330px-Cornwall_Daffodils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yellow